challenge: >
  First notice of loss arrived in five different formats. Adjusters transcribed every claim into the policy admin system before they could work it. Document accuracy mattered: a wrong policy number or date meant rework downstream. The claims SVP had tried an AI vendor pilot that failed evaluation in the first 100 claims. She wanted a system with accuracy gates a claims lead would sign, not a vendor promise.
approach:
  - title: Evaluation-first design
    description: We built the evaluation harness before we built the extraction pipeline. Held-out set of 500 historical FNOLs with ground truth. Accuracy thresholds agreed with the claims lead before development started.
  - title: Document understanding pipeline
    description: Intake across email, fax, and portal into one normalized document store. Extraction with an LLM pipeline, validated against the evaluation harness on every model change.
  - title: Policy admin integration
    description: Idempotent write path into the policy admin system. Audit trail for every claim touch. Human-in-the-loop review for anything that fell below the accuracy threshold.
  - title: Adjuster workflow redesign
    description: Adjuster screen redesigned around the automated draft. Three clicks to accept, one click to escalate, a clear reason code for each.
  - title: Model governance
    description: Model registry, versioning, and quarterly review. Drift monitoring on document types and extracted field accuracy. Runbooks for downgrade and rollback.
outcome: >
  The claims cycle time dropped from 7 days median to 2 days median across the pilot portfolio and held. Eighty percent of first notices processed without manual transcription. Adjuster capacity increased 22% without headcount change. The evaluation harness became the basis for quarterly model reviews with the claims lead signing each one.
outcomeBullets:
